+<body>
+    
+    <main>
+        <div class="eyebrow">AUI-PHY 407 · PLANETARY CONTEXT</div>
+        <h1>One star. Eight worlds. A shared origin.</h1>
+        <p>Explore the planets, build an orbit, and follow the nebular model from cloud to planetary system.</p>
+        <div class="tabs"><button data-tab="system" class="active">Planetary system</button><button
+                data-tab="kepler">Kepler’s laboratory</button><button data-tab="formation">Nebular formation</button>
+        </div>
+        <div class="layout">
+            <section>
+                <div class="card stage"><canvas id="space" role="img"
+                        aria-label="Interactive Solar System and orbital mechanics diagram"></canvas>
+                    <p id="caption" class="caption"></p>
+                </div>
+                <div class="tools"><button id="play">▶ Play</button><button id="reset">Reset time</button><label
+                        for="speed">Time speed</label><select id="speed">
+                        <option value="0.1">0.1 years/s</option>
+                        <option value="1" selected>1 year/s</option>
+                        <option value="5">5 years/s</option>
+                    </select><span id="clock" style="padding:10px;font-size:13px">t = 0.00 years</span></div>
+                <div id="planetButtons" class="choices"></div>
+                <section class="card" id="readout"></section>
+            </section>
+            <aside class="card">
+                <h2 id="controlTitle"></h2>
+                <div id="controls"></div>
+                <div class="metric" id="metric"></div>
+                <h3>Read the model carefully</h3>
+                <p id="limits" style="font-size:13px"></p>
+            </aside>
+        </div>
+        <div class="grid">
+            <section class="card">
+                <div class="eyebrow">COMPARE THE FAMILIES</div>
+                <h2>Inner and outer planets</h2>
+                <div class="tablewrap">
+                    <table>
+                        <thead>
+                            <tr>
+                                <th>Property</th>
+                                <th>Inner terrestrial</th>
+                                <th>Outer giants</th>
+                            </tr>
+                        </thead>
+                        <tbody>
+                            <tr>
+                                <td>Members</td>
+                                <td>Mercury, Venus, Earth, Mars</td>
+                                <td>Jupiter, Saturn, Uranus, Neptune</td>
+                            </tr>
+                            <tr>
+                                <td>Material</td>
+                                <td>Rock and metal dominate</td>
+                                <td>Jupiter/Saturn: H–He dominated; Uranus/Neptune: larger fractions of heavier
+                                    compounds</td>
+                            </tr>
+                            <tr>
+                                <td>Surface</td>
+                                <td>Solid rocky surfaces</td>
+                                <td>No accessible solid surface like Earth's</td>
+                            </tr>
+                            <tr>
+                                <td>Size and density</td>
+                                <td>Smaller; generally higher bulk density</td>
+                                <td>Larger; generally lower bulk density</td>
+                            </tr>
+                            <tr>
+                                <td>Orbits</td>
+                                <td>Smaller semi-major axes and shorter years</td>
+                                <td>Larger semi-major axes and longer years</td>
+                            </tr>
+                            <tr>
+                                <td>Rings and moons</td>
+                                <td>No rings; zero to a few moons</td>
+                                <td>All have rings and multiple moons</td>
+                            </tr>
+                        </tbody>
+                    </table>
+                </div>
+                <p style="font-size:13px">“Ice giant” describes bulk composition, not a planet made of ordinary frozen
+                    ice. The asteroid belt lies mainly between Mars and Jupiter. Pluto is a dwarf planet in the
+                    trans-Neptunian region, not a ninth member of the eight-planet sequence.</p>
+            </section>
+            <section class="card">
+                <div class="eyebrow">WORKED EXAMPLE & PRACTICE</div>
+                <h2>Predict an orbital period.</h2>
+                <p>For a small body orbiting a one-solar-mass star, with a in AU and T in years: T² ≈ a³. At a = 4 AU, T
+                    = √64 = <b>8 years</b>. Eccentricity changes the speed distribution around the orbit, but not this
+                    period at fixed a.</p>
+                <p>What is T, in years, for a = 9 AU around a one-solar-mass star?</p><input class="answer" id="answer"
+                    type="number" aria-label="Orbital period in years"><button id="check" class="primary">Check
+                    answer</button>
+                <p id="feedback" aria-live="polite"></p>
+                <details>
+                    <summary>Hint and solution</summary>
+                    <p>T = a³ᐟ² = 9³ᐟ² = 27 years. For a star of mass M in solar masses and negligible orbiting mass,
+                        use T² = a³/M.</p>
+                </details>
+                <details>
+                    <summary>Investigate and explain</summary>
+                    <p>Choose e = 0.6 in Kepler’s laboratory. Compare the two equal-time sectors near perihelion and
+                        aphelion. Why are their shapes different while their areas are equal? Then double a and check
+                        that T increases by 2³ᐟ², not by 2.</p>
+                </details>
+            </section>
+        </div>
+        <footer>Educational model: no live ephemeris, mutual perturbations, relativistic corrections, orbit inclinations
+            or planetary spin. Positions use arbitrary initial phases. Planet markers and Sun are enlarged independently
+            of orbit distances.<br>Sources: <a href="https://science.nasa.gov/solar-system/planets/">NASA · Planet
+                families</a> · <a href="https://science.nasa.gov/solar-system/orbits-and-keplers-laws/">NASA · Kepler’s
+                laws</a> · <a href="https://science.nasa.gov/solar-system/solar-system-facts/">NASA · Formation</a> · <a
+                href="https://ssd.jpl.nasa.gov/planets/phys_par.html">JPL · Planetary parameters</a>.</footer>
+    </main>
+    <script type="module" src="./solar.js"></script>
+</body>
+
+</html>
